WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.380
Two people in the lab,

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:13.080
a postdoc named Kai Papenfort and a graduate student named Justin Silpe,

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:18.150
were working on Vibrio cholerae because we study quorum sensing in Vibrio cholerae.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.350
And they happened onto a gene,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:22.650
a new gene,

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:24.010
which,

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:28.650
when they were measuring when was Vibrio cholerae expressing that gene. 

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.020
It didn't express it at low cell density, but when cholera grew to high cell density, 

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.550
this gene turned on.

00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:37.300
And in my lab,

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:42.110
when something doesn't happen at low cell density and it turns on at high cell density, we're like, ding ding 

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:43.930
ding! That's quorum sensing,

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.640
right. Bacteria only turn it on when they're in a group.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:47.160
And so we're like,

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:48.070
oh great,

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.550
we have this new quorum sensing gene.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:52.880
So that was fine.

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:57.790
So then Justin and Kai wanted to understand 

00:00:57.790 --> 00:01:00.980
which of the cholerae autoinducers,

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:02.760
which we had discovered long ago,

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:06.620
which of them is most responsible or both,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:06.950
you know,

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:09.330
for turning on that new gene.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.220
So then they made a mutant. It was incapable of making either 

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:18.020
of the known quorum sensing autoinducers.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:23.040
And in that mutant they followed that gene and it turned on at high cell 

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.950
density just fine.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.650
And so that was a eureka moment because they were like,

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:31.050
it's not the autoinducers we know about,

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:36.040
so there had to be something else the cholerae was making, in the absence of the 

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:40.950
two known autoinducers, that was turning on this new gene at high cell density--

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:46.630
so a new autoinducer. And so they reasoned that.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.150
And so then what they did was they grew up huge amounts of cholerae, 

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.080
spun the cells out of the tube, and the autoinducer is on the outside,

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.850
they collected up all that growth medium that the cholerae had grown in,

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.628
this fantasy, you know, mystery autoinducer was in, and they did very 

00:02:04.628 --> 00:02:05.978
standard biochemistry.

00:02:05.978 --> 00:02:08.148
They just fractionated,

00:02:08.148 --> 00:02:08.548
we call it,

00:02:08.548 --> 00:02:13.438
they just made cuts of these growth medium and pared it down and pared it down 

00:02:13.438 --> 00:02:16.508
until finally they had one molecule in their tube.

00:02:16.798 --> 00:02:21.328
They figured out the structure of it and it's this new molecule that we call DPO.

00:02:21.488 --> 00:02:24.618
And so that is our newest autoinducer.

00:02:24.888 --> 00:02:26.828
And so after all of these years,

00:02:28.488 --> 00:02:33.368
there's still many more of these chemical words to find, even in these well studied bacteria,

00:02:34.098 --> 00:02:38.568
and so we're excited to be onto a brand new 

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:43.618
quorum sensing system in this bacterium that we have studied for twenty 

00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:47.938
years and thought we knew a lot about. What's really interesting,

00:02:47.938 --> 00:02:52.288
an interesting twist on quorum sensing in cholerae, is that at high cell density,

00:02:52.498 --> 00:02:55.368
when quorum sensing autoinducers accumulate,

00:02:56.358 --> 00:02:59.838
cholerae shuts off virulence and it tries to get out of the host.

00:03:00.108 --> 00:03:02.388
So if you provide autoinducers,

00:03:03.158 --> 00:03:05.658
cholerae shuts down pathogenecity.

00:03:06.358 --> 00:03:10.168
So now Kai and Justin have a new 

00:03:10.988 --> 00:03:12.528
autoinducer DPO,

00:03:13.658 --> 00:03:18.288
and the dream is that you could just provide that, like I could make a tablet, for 

00:03:18.288 --> 00:03:23.188
example, of DPO, and give it to you and you could take 

00:03:23.188 --> 00:03:26.778
it and that would suppress cholerae virulence.

00:03:29.058 --> 00:03:33.908
The other thing that's really interesting that Kai and Justin showed, is that 

00:03:33.918 --> 00:03:38.508
DPO is not only made, this autoinducer DPO, is not only made 

00:03:39.558 --> 00:03:40.368
by cholerae.

00:03:40.758 --> 00:03:41.208
In fact,

00:03:41.218 --> 00:03:45.808
it's made by many of these microbiome bacteria that live naturally 

00:03:46.348 --> 00:03:47.068
in the gut.

00:03:47.958 --> 00:03:51.658
So what we think, is that the microbiome bacteria are making DPO,

00:03:52.228 --> 00:03:57.148
and that is being used in the gut to fend off this invader, 

00:03:57.298 --> 00:03:57.918
cholerae.

00:03:58.018 --> 00:04:02.462
And so maybe another strategy could be to actually get these 

00:04:02.462 --> 00:04:05.462
microbiome bacteria that live in us naturally,

00:04:06.152 --> 00:04:10.902
to make more DPO or make it on demand, and they could do the fighting 

00:04:11.602 --> 00:04:15.472
against cholerae and it would be a more natural kind of medicine.

00:04:15.472 --> 00:04:17.612
It would be your own commensalor

00:04:17.992 --> 00:04:22.902
mutualistic bacteria fighting off this invader that's not supposed to be 

00:04:22.902 --> 00:04:23.322
there.

00:04:23.492 --> 00:04:27.632
And so those are sort of our two ideas right now for using DPO as a 

00:04:27.782 --> 00:04:31.302
medicine, so it could be DPO or a derivative of DPO,

00:04:31.482 --> 00:04:33.262
a DPO-like molecule.

00:04:33.352 --> 00:04:37.152
And either we're going to make it, hopefully, or we're going to get your bacteria,

00:04:37.162 --> 00:04:38.212
your normal bacteria,

00:04:38.212 --> 00:04:41.162
to make it in the hopes of making people healthier.

